===Etymology 3===
From {{inh|en|enm|lie}}, from {{inh|en|ang|lyġe||lie, falsehood}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*lugiz||lie, falsehood}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*lewgʰ-||to tell lies, swear, complain}}. Cognate with {{cog|osx|luggi||a lie}}, {{cog|goh|lugi|lugī}}, {{m|goh|lugin||a lie}} ({{cog|de|Lüge}}), {{cog|da|løgn||a lie}}, {{cog|bg|лъжа́||а lie}}, {{cog|ru|ложь||а lie}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# An [[intentionally]] [[false]] [[statement]]; an [[intentional]] [[falsehood]].
#: {{syn|en|alternative fact|bullshit|deception|falsehood|fib|leasing|prevarication|Thesaurus:lie}}
#: {{ant|en|truth}}
#: {{ux|en|I knew he was telling a '''lie''' by his facial expression.}}
# A [[statement]] intended to [[deceive]], even if literally true.
#: {{syn|en|half-truth}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} Anything that misleads or disappoints.
#* '''1835''', {{w|Richard Chenevix Trench}}, ''the Story of Justin Martyr''
#*: Wishing this '''lie''' of life was o'er.
#* {{quote-video game|en|year=2007|author={{w|Erik Wolpaw}} and {{w|Chet Faliszek}}|title=[[w:Portal (video game)|Portal]]|text=The cake is a '''lie'''.}}

